'''Hancil Thea''' (born 8 May 1981) is a Burawi politician and businessman serving as the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] since 2025. A member of the '''[[National Party of Burawa]]''', he previously served as a senator representing [[Idri]] in the [[Senate of Burawa]] from 2018 to 2025. His victory in the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] was the first for the National Party in the history of the republic and was achieved through the {{W|ranked-choice voting}} system adopted under the [[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]] endorsed by his predecessor, [[Marik Sahamin]].

Thea rose to national prominence as the principal political representative of the '''[[Dhulsarren]]''', the colloquial name for the agricultural interior of northern Burawa, during the aftermath of the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal|HSR Scandal]] of 2019. His election is regarded as a significant realignment in Burawi politics, marking the first time a candidate from outside the metropolitan establishment won the national leadership.

== Early life and education ==

Hancil Thea was born on 8 May 1981 to a family of {{W|tenant farming|tenant farmers}} in a [[Administrative divisions of Burawa#Rural Administrations|rural administration]] in the [[Dhulsarren]] region of northern [[Burawa]], near the urban administration of [[Idri]]. The Dhulsarren — the agricultural interior of the northern [[Galbeed River|Galbeed]] and [[Sago]] river basins — is the principal grain-producing region of Burawa and a traditional stronghold of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]].

Thea was admitted to the '''[[People's University of Kankadadka]]''', a prestigious institution in the national capital. He was the first member of his immediate family to attend university.

== Early career ==

After completing his education, Thea worked for politicians in [[Kankadadka]], gaining direct exposure to the political establishment. He became disillusioned with the capital's political culture and left government work. He relocated to [[Mazar]], the only city in Burawa where [[Housing in Burawa#Mazar exception|private land ownership]] is permitted, and worked in private industry.

Thea subsequently returned to the Dhulsarren, settling in [[Idri]], near his place of birth. He established a business focused on **agricultural exports**, facilitating the sale of Burawi agricultural produce to international markets. The enterprise connected him to the farming communities of the Dhulsarren and gave him a practical understanding of the economic concerns of rural Burawa — stagnant investment, inadequate infrastructure, and the perception that national policy was formulated for the benefit of the [[Administrative divisions of Burawa#Metropolitan Administrations|eight metropolitan administrations]] at the expense of the interior.

== Political career ==

=== Senator for Idri (2018–2025) ===

Thea entered electoral politics in 2018, winning a seat in the [[Senate of Burawa]] representing the urban administration of [[Idri]]. He stood as a candidate of the [[National Party of Burawa]], the conservative socialist party rooted in the merger of the [[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]] and the [[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa]] in the 1980s.

=== The HSR Scandal and the Dhulsarren crisis ===

In 2019, the '''[[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal]]''' was exposed. [[Cabdirashiid Fure]], a representative from the [[Galbeed River]] area, was found to have conspired with other northern rural representatives to secure billions of [[Burawi Hanti|hanti]] in funding for a parallel [[Burawi High Speed Rail|high-speed rail]] line between [[Sago]] and [[Lanwaley]], justified by falsified population growth projections. The completed route recorded the lowest ridership of any HSR line in the country. Fure and other politicians were arrested.

The government's response extended beyond individual prosecutions. The entire Dhulsarren region was subjected to collective sanctions: funding restrictions, reduced growth allowances, and heightened scrutiny of regional representatives. The measures were perceived within the Dhulsarren as disproportionate collective punishment for the actions of a small number of politicians.

Thea, who had entered the Senate only the year before and had no connection to the scandal, emerged as the principal political voice of the Dhulsarren during the crisis. He consolidated support among the region's remaining representatives and positioned himself as a figure of integrity from a community that had been nationally stigmatised.

=== 2025 leadership election ===
{{main|2025 Burawi leadership election}}

Thea contested the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] as the National Party's candidate. The election was the first conducted under '''{{W|ranked-choice voting}}''', adopted under the [[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]] endorsed during [[Marik Sahamin]]'s second term. Three major candidates stood:

* '''[[Xasan Garaad]]''' ([[Socialist Party of Burawa]]) — 30% first-preference vote
* '''Hancil Thea''' ([[National Party of Burawa]]) — 28% first-preference vote
* '''[[Marik Sahamin]]''' ([[Forward Party of Burawa]]) — 24% first-preference vote

Under the RCV redistribution, the National Party proved to be the overwhelming second-preference choice for voters of all other parties. Voters who preferred the Socialist or Forward parties were reluctant to rank the other metropolitan party second, but accepted the National Party — which occupied a distinct geographic and ideological position — as an alternative. Thea accumulated sufficient second-preference votes to surpass Garaad and was elected [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]].

The result was widely described as an upset. The National Party had never previously won a leadership election, and Thea's personal trajectory — from tenant farmer's son to senator to national leader — was characterised in Burawi media as a narrative of the Dhulsarren' political rehabilitation.

== Leader of the Burawi Republic ==

=== Divided government ===

Thea assumed office facing a '''divided government'''. The [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] and its coalition partners held approximately 45% of seats in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]], forming a minority government under Prime Minister [[Iacob Naq]] of the Socialist Party. The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] retained a further bloc in the Lower House and continued to control the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]], which operated on a separate three-year electoral cycle and had not been contested in 2025. The National Party lacked a legislative majority in either chamber, and Thea's ability to advance his agenda through parliament was accordingly constrained.

The [[President of Burawa|President]], [[Nacan Badalgen]] of the Forward Party, had been nominated by Sahamin during the previous term. Badalgen's five-year term was due to expire, granting Thea the authority to nominate a successor subject to parliamentary confirmation.

=== Domestic policy ===

Thea's early policy actions were concentrated on **rural investment** and the reversal of measures perceived as disadvantageous to the interior.

The most prominent initiative was the reopening of '''domestic air routes'''. The previous government had passed the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]], banning domestic commercial flights between all but the major city airports and directing passenger traffic to the [[Burawi High Speed Rail|high-speed rail]] network. The act had been a persistent grievance in rural Burawa, where residents were required to travel to metropolitan airports before accessing the rail network. Unable to secure a legislative repeal in a parliament dominated by the Socialist and Forward parties, Thea employed his executive authority to direct the [[Burawi Armed Forces|military]] to open military airfields for domestic civilian use and to instruct the state-owned [[national airline]] to recommence domestic routes. The directive drew criticism from opposition parties, who characterised it as militarisation of civilian infrastructure and circumvention of parliamentary authority.

=== Commutation of Cabdirashiid Fure ===

In February 2026, Thea commuted the sentence of [[Cabdirashiid Fure]], the former representative who had been the principal figure in the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal|HSR Scandal]] of 2019. The commutation reduced Fure's prison term to time served. The action was denounced by opposition parties as interference with judicial process and protection of corrupt officials, and as symbolic of Thea's alignment with the rural interests implicated in the scandal.

=== 2026 recall vote ===

{{main|2026 Burawi leadership recall vote}}

On 13 March 2026, Parliament voted on a motion to recall Thea from office under Article 47 of the [[Constitution of Burawa]]. The motion, filed jointly by the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] and the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] and supported by a coalition of opposition parties, required approval by seventy-five per cent of Parliament. The motion received 754 votes in favour, representing approximately seventy-three and one-half per cent, falling 16 votes short of the 770 required for recall. The [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]], which maintained an informal conservative alliance with the National Party, voted against the recall, while the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] abstained pending negotiations with the Thea administration. A second recall vote was scheduled for 20 March 2026.

== Political positions ==

Thea's politics are rooted in the '''conservative socialist''' tradition of the [[National Party of Burawa]]. He advocates for increased investment in rural infrastructure, agricultural modernisation, and the restoration of services to non-metropolitan communities. His platform reflects the National Party's historical base in the merger of the [[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]] and the [[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa]], combining social conservatism with agrarian economic priorities.

== Personal life ==

Thea was born and raised in the [[Dhulsarren]] region of northern Burawa. He is a graduate of the [[People's University of Kankadadka]].

